solution_steps:
The triangle with vertices $\mathbf{0},$ $\mathbf{a},$ and $\mathbf{b}$ is formed by the vectors $\mathbf{a}$ and $\mathbf{b}.$

[asy]
unitsize(0.5 cm);

pair A, B, O;

A = (3,1);
B = (-5,2);
O = (0,0);

draw(O--A--B--cycle);
draw((-7,0)--(4,0));
draw((0,-1)--(0,3));
draw(O--A,Arrow(6));
draw(O--B,Arrow(6));

label("$\mathbf{a}$", (A + O)/2, N);
label("$\mathbf{b}$", (B + O)/2, N);
label("$\mathbf{0}$", O, SW);
[/asy]

The area of a triangle formed by two vectors is given by half the magnitude of the cross product of those vectors. In this case, the area of the triangle is given by

$$\frac{1}{2}|\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b}|.$$

The cross product of $\mathbf{a}$ and $\mathbf{b}$ is given by

$$\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} = \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
3 & 1 & 0 \\
-5 & 2 & 0 \\
\end{vmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
0 \\
3(2) - 1(-5) \\
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
0 \\
11 \\
\end{pmatrix}.$$

Therefore,

$$\frac{1}{2}|\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b}| = \frac{1}{2}|\begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
0 \\
11 \\
\end{pmatrix}| = \frac{1}{2} \cdot 11 = \boxed{\frac{11}{2}}.$$

The answer is: \frac{11}{2}

